{"timestamp": "2026-10-18T20:18:08.360162", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Threonine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\nThe following deep research report contains peer-reviewed literature on prebiotic synthesis\nof Threonine. Use it as evidence to inform your network design.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of Threonine: Routes, Intermediates, Catalysts, Environments, and Unresolved Problems\n\n## Scope and overall assessment\n\nThreonine is a comparatively difficult proteinogenic amino acid to account for prebiotically because its four-carbon skeleton contains **two adjacent stereogenic centers and a \u03b2-hydroxyl group**. The literature nevertheless contains several genuinely distinct routes to threonine or to chemically immediate threonine precursors. They span aqueous HCN chemistry, Strecker chemistry, aldehyde\u2013amino-acid condensation, atmospheric electrical discharge, ionizing-radiation chemistry, and extraterrestrial ice photochemistry. The evidentiary strength is very uneven: some experiments isolated or chromatographically identified threonine after hydrolysis, whereas others stopped at lactaldehyde or the threonine aminonitrile and therefore established a **precursor route rather than free threonine itself**.\n\nThe strongest threonine-specific conclusions from the primary literature are:\n\n| Route class | What has actually been demonstrated? | Main feedstocks | Evidentiary status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Cyanosulfidic/Kiliani\u2013Fischer\u2013Strecker sequence | Lactaldehyde in **19% yield**, followed by conversion to the threonine/allo-threonine aminonitrile | HCN-derived nitriles, H\u2082S, NH\u2083, UV; Cu(I) in the original implementation | **Strong precursor-level demonstration**; free-threonine hydrolysis was not the central endpoint of the experiment. |\n| Aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde | D,L-threonine and allo-threonine after hydrolysis/decarboxylation | HCN trimer + CH\u2083CHO | **Direct experimental threonine route** under mild aqueous conditions. |\n| Glycine/oligoglycine + acetaldehyde | Threonine among products | Glycine or glycine oligomers + CH\u2083CHO | **Direct experimental route**, catalyst-free, pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C, but poorly selective. |\n| Concentrated HCN/NH\u2083 polymer chemistry | Threonine released from hydrolyzed HCN-derived material | HCN or NH\u2083/HCN systems | **Historical direct detection after strong hydrolysis**; chemical identity of the immediate precursor is poorly constrained. |\n| \u03b3-radiolysis of aqueous HCN/cyanide | Threonine after hydrolysis of irradiated material | HCN, or NH\u2084CN/NaCN + ionizing radiation | **Direct post-hydrolysis detection**, independently reproduced in the 1970s. |\n| Electric-discharge chemistry | Threonine in the H\u2082S-rich 1958 Miller experiment | CH\u2084, NH\u2083, CO\u2082, H\u2082S, H\u2082O + spark | **Direct product-level detection**, but pathway and environmental realism are uncertain. |\n| UV photolysis of interstellar ice analogues | Threonine and allothreonine after ice photochemistry/workup | H\u2082O, C\u2082H\u2085OH, HCN, NH\u2083 | **Direct laboratory production**, with Thr:Gly = 60:100 in one ethanol-ice experiment. |\n| PAH-containing astrophysical ice | Threonine detected at relative abundance 13.6 versus alanine = 100; quantum yield \\(1.1\\times10^{-6}\\) | Naphthalene, H\u2082O, NH\u2083 + UV/EUV | **Direct detection**, but extremely low absolute conversion. |\n| Interstellar lactaldehyde synthesis | Lactaldehyde made from CO + ethanol ice | CO, C\u2082H\u2085OH + energetic electrons | **Upstream precursor only**; strongly relevant because lactaldehyde is the required C\u2083 aldehyde for Strecker threonine. |\n| Aldehyde/ammonia \u201cformose-like\u201d parent-body chemistry | Many hydroxy amino acids, but **not threonine/allo-threonine** except trace L-Thr attributed to contamination | HCHO, CH\u2083CHO, NH\u2083, H\u2082O, \u00b1 glycolaldehyde | **Important negative result** for threonine. |\n| \u201cComplex glycine precursor\u201d alkylation | Acetaldehyde proposed as the side-chain donor for Thr | Irradiation-derived glycine precursor + CH\u2083CHO | **Proposed, not demonstrated threonine synthesis**; currently speculative. |\n\nTwo distinctions are crucial for synthesis-network design. First, **\u201cthreonine detected after 6 M HCl hydrolysis\u201d is not equivalent to \u201cfree threonine formed in the prebiotic reactor.\u201d** Several HCN, radiation, and ice experiments instead generate nitriles, oligomers, or other bound precursors that release threonine analytically. Second, the best-defined carbon-skeleton chemistry converges repeatedly on **acetaldehyde, lactaldehyde, glycine, or HCN oligomers**, making these more useful nodes for a reaction-network model than \u201cthreonine formation\u201d treated as a single black-box edge.\n\n## Known synthesis routes and their mechanisms\n\n### Cyanosulfidic photoredox chemistry through lactaldehyde\n\nThe most mechanistically explicit modern route was developed by **Dougal Ritson and John Sutherland (2013, *Angewandte Chemie International Edition*)** and incorporated into the broader cyanosulfidic network by **Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry*)**. Its central idea is a photochemical, reductive version of Kiliani\u2013Fischer homologation in which an HCN-derived nitrile is reduced to an aldehyde, the aldehyde adds another HCN unit, and the new cyanohydrin is reduced again.\n\nFor threonine, the chemically important branch is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_3CHO + HCN \\rightarrow CH_3CH(OH)CN}\n\\]\n\nacetaldehyde \u2192 **lactonitrile**, followed by photoreductive conversion of the nitrile carbon to an aldehyde:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_3CH(OH)CN \\rightarrow CH_3CH(OH)CHO}\n\\]\n\ngiving **lactaldehyde (2-hydroxypropanal)**. Lactaldehyde then enters ordinary Strecker chemistry:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_3CH(OH)CHO + HCN + NH_3\n\\rightleftharpoons\nCH_3CH(OH)CH(NH_2)CN}\n\\]\n\nfollowed, in principle, by nitrile hydrolysis to:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_3CH(OH)CH(NH_2)COOH}\n\\]\n\nwhich is threonine/allothreonine. Ritson and Sutherland explicitly obtained lactaldehyde from the acetaldehyde-cyanohydrin system in **19% yield after 2 h**, and subsequently showed that addition of HCN and excess NH\u2083 converted lactaldehyde through its cyanohydrin into the **(allo-)threonine Strecker aminonitrile**. Conversion of related aldehydes to aminonitriles proceeded over days to weeks.\n\nThe underlying photoreduction was demonstrated under approximately neutral aqueous conditions. In a representative first-stage experiment, Ritson and Sutherland used **10 mM glycolonitrile, 30 mM H\u2082S, 33 mM phosphate, 10 mol% CuCN, pH 7, and 254-nm irradiation for 2 h**, obtaining glycolaldehyde hydrate in **42% yield**. The same photochemical logic generated acetaldehyde and ultimately lactaldehyde. H\u2082S acts as a stoichiometric reductant while UV excitation produces the reducing chemistry; Cu(I) greatly facilitates the original photoredox implementation.\n\nPatel et al. subsequently showed that this chemistry belongs to a larger network in which HCN-derived carbon can furnish precursors of ribonucleotides, amino acids and lipids. Importantly, in H\u2082S-containing versions, Cu(I) is not always indispensable to reduction, so copper should be regarded as a demonstrated accelerator/photoredox component rather than an invariant stoichiometric requirement of every version of the network.\n\nA later alternative photoredox system was demonstrated by **Xu, Ritson, Ranjan, Todd, Sasselov and Sutherland (2018, *Chemical Communications*)**: UV irradiation of **ferrocyanide plus sulfite** efficiently drives HCN reductive homologation to simple sugars and amino-acid/hydroxy-acid precursors. This increases the geochemical flexibility of the reductive-homologation module, although Xu et al. did **not establish a new threonine endpoint comparable to Ritson and Sutherland's lactaldehyde/aminonitrile experiment**.\n\nThe strengths of this route are unusually clear intermediates, a measured lactaldehyde yield, aqueous conditions near neutral pH, and a common chemical network with other classes of prebiotic molecules. Its weaknesses are equally clear: the endpoint experimentally emphasized is the **aminonitrile precursor**, not an isolated yield of free threonine; the route requires coordinated supplies of cyanide, reductant, UV, ammonia and sometimes copper; and there are geochemical tensions between abundant sulfide and UV transmission, discussed below.\n\n### Aminomalononitrile plus acetaldehyde\n\nA chemically distinct HCN route was demonstrated by **John W. Thanassi (1975, *Journal of Molecular Evolution*)**. Aminomalononitrile, NH\u2082CH(CN)\u2082, is the **HCN trimer**. Thanassi found that it reacts with electrophiles, including aldehydes, under mild aqueous conditions; acid hydrolysis of the products yielded several amino acids, explicitly including **D,L-threonine and allo-threonine**.\n\nFor threonine the electrophile is **acetaldehyde**. The reaction can be represented schematically as nucleophilic C\u2013C bond formation at the activated carbon of aminomalononitrile:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{NH_2CH(CN)_2 + CH_3CHO\n\\rightarrow\nNH_2C(CN)_2CH(OH)CH_3}\n\\]\n\nfollowed by conversion of both nitriles into carboxyl groups under acid hydrolysis and loss of one carboxyl group by decarboxylation:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{NH_2C(COOH)_2CH(OH)CH_3\n\\rightarrow\nNH_2CH(COOH)CH(OH)CH_3 + CO_2}\n\\]\n\nA later experimental paper by **Kuroda et al. (2020, *Natural Product Communications*)**, while extending the aminomalononitrile concept to other amino acids, describes Thanassi's threonine reaction as occurring by **alkylation with acetaldehyde under almost neutral aqueous conditions**, followed by acid hydrolysis and decarboxylation. Thanassi's primary abstract reports that the products were quantitatively measured but the numerical threonine yield is not given in the accessible abstract, so it would be unsafe to invent a percentage.\n\nNo mineral or metal catalyst is required for the demonstrated C\u2013C-forming step. The route therefore has an attractive minimality: **HCN \u2192 HCN trimer; HCN trimer + acetaldehyde \u2192 threonine precursor; hydrolysis/decarboxylation \u2192 threonine**. Its main liabilities are upstream. It requires enough HCN to generate and retain aminomalononitrile, acetaldehyde must be available simultaneously, and the laboratory endpoint uses strong acid hydrolysis. It also produces threonine together with its allo stereoisomer rather than solving threonine's stereochemical-selection problem.\n\nFor network modeling, aminomalononitrile is particularly interesting because it provides a **C\u2082N\u2083 activated carbon synthon generated directly by HCN oligomerization**, rather than requiring lactaldehyde. It therefore constitutes a genuinely independent branch from the lactaldehyde/Strecker route even though both ultimately depend on cyanide chemistry.\n\n### Condensation of glycine with acetaldehyde\n\nA much less frequently discussed but experimentally important route was reported by **C. P. Ivanov and O. C. Ivanov (1983, *Origins of Life*)**. They reacted **glycine and glycine oligohomopeptides with formaldehyde and acetaldehyde** in homogeneous weakly acidic solution. The reactions were run at **pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C, explicitly in the absence of inorganic solids**. Among the products were the expected serine and **threonine**, together with alanine, glutamate, aspartate, norvaline, isoleucine and nonprotein hydroxy amino acids.\n\nThe simplest net threonine transformation is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{glycine + acetaldehyde \\rightarrow threonine/allothreonine}\n\\]\n\nThat C\u2013C connectivity is the reverse of the familiar retro-aldol cleavage of threonine to glycine plus acetaldehyde. The prebiotic experiment is especially significant because it does **not require cyanide in the threonine-forming step, metal minerals, UV or ionizing radiation**. Glycine can in principle be supplied by many other prebiotic routes, so this reaction creates a useful point of convergence between otherwise unrelated origin-of-life networks.\n\nThe trade-off is selectivity. The Ivanovs obtained a fairly broad amino-acid inventory, indicating that aldehyde\u2013amino-acid chemistry in this regime is not a selective threonine synthesis. The accessible primary abstract gives no isolated threonine percentage or threonine:allothreonine ratio. More fundamentally, the route moves rather than eliminates the precursor problem: it requires a pool containing **glycine or oligoglycine plus acetaldehyde at 60\u201380 \u00b0C**.\n\nNevertheless, among routes relevant to network design it deserves more attention than it commonly receives because it provides a direct edge:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{glycine}+\\text{acetaldehyde}\\rightarrow\\text{threonine-family products}}\n\\]\n\nunder catalyst-free aqueous conditions.\n\n### Bulk HCN and ammonia chemistry\n\nThreonine also appears in the older literature on **HCN oligomerization/polymerization**, where the immediate molecular precursor is much less well defined.\n\n**Lowe, Rees and Markham (1963, *Nature*)** demonstrated that ammonium cyanide produces a complex collection of amino acids, amino-acid polymers, fatty acids and purines. Closely related work by **Clifford Matthews and Robert Moser (1966\u20131967, *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences* and *Nature*)** showed that HCN-derived polymers and HCN/ammonia reaction products can, after hydrolysis, yield a broad suite of \u03b1-amino acids. Matthews and Moser's hydrolysates explicitly contained **threonine**, alongside glycine, serine, aspartate, histidine, lysine and other amino acids.\n\nThe key mechanistic point is that this is **not clean Strecker chemistry from a known carbonyl**. HCN produces dimers, trimers, tetramers and higher nitrogen-rich oligomers; water and ammonia then transform these networks into amide-, peptide- and amino-acid-like structures. Matthews and Moser emphasized peptide-like material that released amino acids only after strong hydrolysis. Thus the experiment proves that an HCN/NH\u2083 network can encode the threonine carbon skeleton, but does not identify a single dominant elementary pathway to it.\n\nThis ambiguity is important. A reactor that yields threonine only after **6 N HCl at approximately 100 \u00b0C for many hours** has demonstrated a hydrolyzable threonine precursor, not necessarily an environmental pool of free threonine. Moreover, classical HCN-polymer experiments commonly use very high cyanide concentrations and alkaline/ammoniacal conditions, making concentration a major prebiotic-plausibility issue. The chemistry is real; its geochemical deployment remains contested.\n\n### Ionizing-radiation chemistry of HCN\n\n**Sweeney, Toste and Ponnamperuma (1976, *Origins of Life*)** exposed **0.004\u20130.1 M aqueous HCN** to ^60Co \u03b3-radiation. After irradiation and hydrolysis they identified seven protein amino acids by chromatographic/mass-spectrometric methods: glycine, alanine, valine, serine, **threonine**, aspartate and glutamate. The reported ^60Co dose rate was approximately **200,000 rad h\u207b\u00b9**, equivalent to about **2 kGy h\u207b\u00b9**. Control experiments were used to exclude contamination.\n\n**Zorica Dragani\u0107, Ivan Dragani\u0107, Akira Shimoyama and Cyril Ponnamperuma (1977, *Origins of Life*)** subsequently irradiated oxygen-free aqueous HCN, NH\u2084CN and NaCN and concluded that the process is initiated by **free-radical chemistry producing oligomeric products that release amino acids on hydrolysis**. Chiral products were approximately racemic, and radiation-chemical amino-acid yields varied with radiation dose.\n\nThis route needs no mineral catalyst: the source of activation is ionizing radiation and water-derived/cyanide-derived radical chemistry. Its principal prebiotic uncertainty is therefore not chemical feasibility but **dosimetry**. A ^60Co source is a laboratory proxy, not a natural Hadean source; demonstrating that an equivalent dose can be supplied at useful rates by radionuclides, energetic particles, impact radiation or another natural process remains necessary before the laboratory route can be promoted to a quantitatively compelling terrestrial scenario. The analytical-hydrolysis caveat also applies: threonine was recovered after hydrolysis of irradiated products.\n\n### Electric-discharge synthesis\n\nThe best direct atmospheric-discharge evidence comes from the archived 1958 experiment reanalyzed by **Eric Parker and colleagues (2011, *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences*)**. Miller had charged a 5-L apparatus with approximately **258 mmHg CH\u2084, 100 mmHg H\u2082S, 250 mmHg NH\u2083 and 87 mmHg CO\u2082**, then subjected the recirculating system to electrical discharge. Modern UPLC and time-of-flight MS analysis found **23 amino acids and four amines**. Threonine, leucine and isoleucine were detected in this H\u2082S-rich experiment but not in the comparison classic, volcanic or silent-discharge sample sets examined by the same group.\n\nNo intentional mineral catalyst was present. However, Parker et al. noted sulfur deposition near the **tungsten electrodes**, leaving open the possibility that electrode/sulfur surface chemistry modified product distributions. Consequently, it is safest to describe the experiment as **spark-driven rather than rigorously catalyst-free**. The detailed pathway to threonine was not established; plausible chemistry includes formation of carbonyls, HCN and nitriles followed by aqueous reactions, but assigning a particular threonine mechanism would go beyond the experiment.\n\nThe result is nevertheless important because it demonstrates that threonine can emerge from a complex gas-phase/aqueous reaction network without supplying lactaldehyde or aminomalononitrile by hand. The drawback is environmental. The experiment is strongly reducing and unusually rich in CH\u2084, NH\u2083 and H\u2082S compared with most models of the global Hadean atmosphere. Parker et al. themselves therefore emphasized possible **local or transient reducing environments**, including volcanic settings, rather than requiring such a composition globally.\n\nA separate historical line of work by **Matthews and Moser (1966, *PNAS*)** subjected dry CH\u2084/NH\u2083 mixtures to electrical discharge and found threonine among amino acids released by hydrolysis of resulting peptide-like residues. This configuration differs chemically from the canonical Miller water-circulation experiment and again points to a complex hydrolyzable precursor network rather than a uniquely resolved threonine-forming elementary reaction.\n\n### Extraterrestrial ice photochemistry\n\nAstrophysical ice chemistry provides two separate direct threonine demonstrations.\n\n**Jamie Elsila, Jason Dworkin, Max Bernstein, Mildred Martin and Scott Sandford (2007, *The Astrophysical Journal*)** irradiated interstellar-ice analogues at approximately **20 K**. In an experiment replacing methanol with ethanol, the starting ice had composition:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{H_2O:C_2H_5OH:HCN:NH_3=20:2:1:1}.\n\\]\n\nThe overall amino-acid yield was lower than in the corresponding methanol system: glycine was only about **10% of the glycine yield in the methanol-containing ice**. But the carbon-chain diversity increased substantially. In the hydrolyzed residue, relative abundances normalized to glycine = 100 included **D,L-threonine = 60 and D,L-allothreonine = 30**, along with alanine = 26, \u03b2-alanine = 5, serine = 14 and several other products.\n\nThis is one of the strongest quantitative threonine results in the entire literature. It is also mechanistically sobering. Isotope-labeling experiments in the same study showed that amino-acid formation in UV-processed ices proceeds through **multiple pathways**, and for the compounds investigated in detail the dominant routes did not conform simply to either a textbook Strecker mechanism or the simplest radical\u2013radical scheme. Threonine's own detailed atom-by-atom pathway was not resolved.\n\nA second experiment by **Y.-J. Chen, Michel Nuevo and colleagues (2008, *Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society*)** irradiated a **1:1:1 naphthalene:H\u2082O:NH\u2083 ice at 15 K** with **4\u201320 eV photons**. Thirteen amino acids were identified. Alanine was most abundant at an extrapolated **2.2 nmol** in the entire sample; threonine had a relative abundance of **13.6 when alanine = 100** and an estimated photochemical quantum yield of **\\(1.1\\times10^{-6}\\)**.\n\nThe naphthalene experiment is relevant to broad surveys because it demonstrates a threonine route that does **not begin with HCN as the supplied carbon source**. Its limitation is efficiency: only a tiny fraction of the PAH carbon entered identified amino acids, and many other organic photoproducts dominated the residue. The authors therefore interpreted it as evidence for multiple possible astrochemical mechanisms, not a selective threonine factory.\n\n### Interstellar production of the critical lactaldehyde precursor\n\nA major advance relevant to the Strecker route came from **Wang et al. (2024, *Nature Communications*)**, who directly synthesized **lactaldehyde** in an interstellar-ice analogue. A CO/ethanol ice at **5 K** was exposed to energetic electrons as proxies for galactic cosmic rays. The experimentally supported elementary step is radical\u2013radical combination:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{HCO^\\bullet + CH_3CHOH^\\bullet\n\\rightarrow CH_3CH(OH)CHO}\n\\]\n\nthat is, formyl + 1-hydroxyethyl \u2192 lactaldehyde. Because radical\u2013radical recombination is barrierless once the partners meet, the chemistry is viable at extremely low temperature. Lactaldehyde was identified using isomer-selective photoionization mass spectrometry and isotopic substitution.\n\nThis experiment does **not make threonine**, but it solves an important upstream problem independently of terrestrial cyanosulfidic chemistry. Lactaldehyde made in molecular-cloud/protoplanetary ice could in principle be incorporated into asteroids or comets, undergo aqueous Strecker chemistry with HCN/NH\u2083 during parent-body alteration, or be delivered to an early planetary surface. Thus two conceptually different frameworks now converge on the same high-value network node, lactaldehyde:\n\n\\[\n\\text{surface HCN photochemistry} \\rightarrow \\text{lactaldehyde}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\text{CO/ethanol interstellar ice chemistry} \\rightarrow \\text{lactaldehyde}.\n\\]\n\n### Proposed complex-glycine-precursor route\n\n**Kuroda and Kobayashi (2024, *Molecules*)** proposed a broader \u201ccomplex glycine precursor\u201d or CGP framework in which energetic processing first creates a polymeric/complex glycine precursor and subsequent side-chain alkylation generates proteinogenic amino-acid skeletons. In their threonine scheme, **acetaldehyde is the proposed side-chain precursor**. The authors discuss hydrogen bonding as a possible source of preference for threonine over allothreonine.\n\nThis route must presently be kept separate from experimentally established threonine syntheses. The authors themselves identify kinetic and mechanistic uncertainties, and a complete CGP \u2192 threonine transformation with measured yield was not demonstrated. It is therefore useful as a **network hypothesis**, particularly because it again makes acetaldehyde a central C\u2082 precursor, but it should not be assigned the evidentiary weight of the Thanassi, Ivanov, Ritson\u2013Sutherland, Parker or ice experiments.\n\n## Intermediates, catalysts, and quantitative experimental evidence\n\nAcross otherwise very different frameworks, only a relatively small number of intermediates repeatedly matter.\n\n**Acetaldehyde (CH\u2083CHO)** is the most conspicuous hub. It supplies the methyl-bearing carbon unit in the aminomalononitrile reaction, reacts with HCN to produce lactonitrile in the cyanosulfidic route, reacts directly with glycine/oligoglycine in the Ivanov route, and is the proposed side-chain reagent in the CGP hypothesis. It also occurs naturally among products of energetic prebiotic chemistry.\n\n**Lactonitrile, CH\u2083CH(OH)CN**, is the key reduced-homologation intermediate linking acetaldehyde to lactaldehyde. **Lactaldehyde, CH\u2083CH(OH)CHO**, is then the direct carbonyl precursor required by a conventional Strecker construction of the threonine skeleton. The final immediate Strecker precursor is **2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile**, whose nitrile carbon becomes threonine's carboxyl carbon upon hydrolysis.\n\n**Aminomalononitrile, NH\u2082CH(CN)\u2082**, provides an alternative HCN-derived route in which the amino functionality and activated \u03b1-carbon are already present before acetaldehyde addition. This route bypasses lactaldehyde altogether.\n\n**Glycine** is the minimal amino-acid precursor in the Ivanov chemistry. Since glycine itself is among the easiest amino acids to obtain from discharge, HCN, Strecker and extraterrestrial chemistry, the glycine + acetaldehyde reaction could potentially act as a downstream convergence step rather than requiring a unique dedicated route from inorganic gases to threonine.\n\nA compact comparison of experimentally imposed conditions illustrates how different the routes actually are:\n\n| Study | Reaction conditions | Catalyst/energy | Threonine-specific outcome |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Ritson & Sutherland 2013, Angew. Chem. Int. Ed.** | Representative photoreduction: ~10 mM nitrile, 30 mM H\u2082S, 33 mM phosphate, pH 7, 254 nm; CuCN ~10 mol%; 2 h | UV + H\u2082S; Cu(I) photoredox | Lactaldehyde **19%**; subsequent HCN/NH\u2083 gave Thr/allo-Thr aminonitrile. |\n| **Thanassi 1975, J. Mol. Evol.** | Mild, approximately neutral aqueous reaction of aminomalononitrile with acetaldehyde; subsequent acid hydrolysis/decarboxylation | **No mineral catalyst required** | D,L-threonine + allo-threonine; paper states products were quantified, but accessible abstract does not expose numeric yield. |\n| **Ivanov & Ivanov 1983, Origins of Life** | pH **3.3\u20133.7**, **60\u201380 \u00b0C**, glycine/oligoglycine + HCHO/CH\u2083CHO | Explicitly **no inorganic solids** | Threonine among several protein and nonprotein amino acids. |\n| **Sweeney, Toste & Ponnamperuma 1976, Origins of Life** | HCN **0.004\u20130.1 M**, ^60Co irradiation, about **200,000 rad h\u207b\u00b9** | \u03b3-radiation; no conventional catalyst | Thr among seven protein amino acids after hydrolysis. |\n| **Parker et al. 2011, PNAS** | CH\u2084/H\u2082S/NH\u2083/CO\u2082 \u2248 258/100/250/87 mmHg in Miller apparatus | Electrical spark; tungsten electrodes | Thr detected; absent from three comparison Miller sample sets. |\n| **Elsila et al. 2007, ApJ** | H\u2082O:C\u2082H\u2085OH:HCN:NH\u2083 = **20:2:1:1**, ~20 K | UV photolysis of ice | Hydrolysate: Gly = 100, Thr = **60**, allo-Thr = **30**. |\n| **Chen et al. 2008, MNRAS** | C\u2081\u2080H\u2088:H\u2082O:NH\u2083 = **1:1:1**, 15 K, 4\u201320 eV photons | UV/EUV | Thr relative abundance **13.6** vs Ala = 100; quantum yield **1.1\u00d710\u207b\u2076**. |\n| **Wang et al. 2024, Nat. Commun.** | CO/ethanol ice, **5 K**, electron irradiation | Energetic electrons/cosmic-ray proxy | Lactaldehyde, not Thr; barrierless HCO\u2022 + CH\u2083CHOH\u2022 recombination. |\n\nSeveral conclusions follow from the catalyst literature.\n\nThere is **no universal catalytic requirement** for threonine chemistry. Aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde and glycine/acetaldehyde reactions work without minerals; \u03b3-radiolysis and astrophysical-ice routes are energy-driven; discharge chemistry likewise does not require an intentionally added mineral.\n\nCopper has the clearest demonstrated catalytic role in the threonine-specific modern surface chemistry. CuCN/Cu(I) participates in photoredox cycling that accelerates nitrile reductions in the Ritson\u2013Sutherland system. However, H\u2082S-driven versions can function without Cu(I), and **sulfite/ferrocyanide** offers a distinct photoredox implementation of HCN reductive homologation. It would therefore be incorrect to encode \u201cCuCN required\u201d as an absolute dependency for every HCN-to-hydroxyaldehyde network.\n\nNo clay, FeS, NiS or alkaline-hydrothermal mineral has yet emerged from the threonine-specific literature as an experimentally established catalyst comparable to copper in the photoredox route. Generic amino-acid syntheses on minerals or in hydrothermal systems exist, but that is not equivalent to demonstrating threonine formation; the target-specific evidence surveyed here does not justify adding a \u201chydrothermal mineral \u2192 threonine\u201d edge merely by analogy.\n\nA final analytical caution is essential. **Strong acid hydrolysis is itself a chemical transformation.** In HCN-polymer, radiolysis, and several ice experiments, it converts nitriles, amides or oligomer-bound precursors into measurable amino acids. Such experiments establish that the irradiation or polymerization network contained the appropriate carbon\u2013nitrogen framework, but not necessarily that free threonine accumulated before laboratory hydrolysis. Dragani\u0107 et al.'s finding that irradiated cyanide gives oligomers that release amino acids is particularly explicit on this point.\n\n## Environmental contexts and geochemical plausibility\n\n### Sunlit surface waters, ponds, and evaporative basins\n\nOf the terrestrial environments, shallow surface waters are the closest fit to the Ritson\u2013Sutherland chemistry because the reactions need UV radiation, liquid water, cyanide and a reducing sulfur species. The difficult issue is not whether the organic reactions work but whether all necessary reagents can coexist at useful concentrations.\n\nThere is meaningful geochemical support for concentrating cyanide. **Jonathan Toner and David Catling (2019, *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*)** modeled closed-basin alkaline lakes and experimentally constrained precipitation of **sodium ferrocyanide salts** over plausible early-Earth conditions. Evaporation and mineral weathering can stockpile atmospheric HCN as ferrocyanide; subsequent heating of sediments can release concentrated cyanide salts. This provides a concrete geological mechanism for overcoming at least part of the \u201cdilute HCN\u201d problem.\n\nThe sulfide side is more contentious. **Sukrit Ranjan, Zoe Todd, John Sutherland and Dimitar Sasselov (2018, *Astrobiology*)** calculated early-Earth sulfide speciation and concluded that substantial aqueous HS\u207b concentrations are generally difficult to maintain because metal ions precipitate sulfides and because atmospheric sulfur species affect UV transmission. They explicitly identified a possible tension in cyanosulfidic scenarios: H\u2082S/SO\u2082 can provide sulfur-derived reducing species, but abundant sulfur gases/aerosols can also attenuate the UV needed to drive photochemistry. The authors did identify local geochemical circumstances\u2014and sulfite/bisulfite chemistry\u2014that can partly alleviate the problem, so this is a constraint rather than a disproof.\n\nAccordingly, a **localized, periodically concentrated surface setting** is better supported than a homogeneous \u201cprimordial ocean\u201d for this route. Evaporation, cyanide storage, water-level cycling and spatial separation could provide concentrations that open-ocean chemistry cannot. But no single field site or geochemical model has yet demonstrated simultaneous natural availability at laboratory-effective levels of HCN, ammonia, sulfur reductant, phosphate and photoredox catalyst.\n\n### Reducing atmospheres and volcanic environments\n\nThe Miller H\u2082S experiment establishes excellent **chemical possibility** but weaker global environmental plausibility. Modern discussions generally favor an early atmosphere substantially richer in N\u2082 and CO\u2082 than the highly reducing CH\u2084/NH\u2083 atmosphere used in classic Miller chemistry. **Cleaves, Chalmers, Lazcano, Miller and Bada (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*)** nevertheless showed that even neutral N\u2082/CO\u2082 discharge mixtures can make significant amino acids if nitrite/nitrate-mediated destruction during workup is suppressed, for example by ferrous iron. Their result weakens the simplistic claim that atmospheric amino-acid synthesis requires a permanently methane-rich planet, although it did not specifically establish threonine production from neutral gas mixtures.\n\nFor threonine itself, the strongest discharge result remains Parker et al.'s H\u2082S-rich experiment, whose CH\u2084/NH\u2083/H\u2082S inventory is more plausibly treated as **local or transient**\u2014for example around reducing volcanic plumes\u2014than as a global atmospheric composition.\n\nImpact-generated reducing atmospheres provide another way to generate temporary H\u2082/CH\u2084/HCN-rich conditions. Modern atmospheric modeling finds that large impacts can produce reducing episodes lasting roughly **10\u2075\u201310\u2077 years**, with HCN production rising sharply when CH\u2084/CO\u2082 becomes sufficiently high. Such models do not synthesize threonine directly, but they provide a plausible upstream feedstock source for the HCN-dependent threonine routes.\n\nThus the atmospheric framework is best viewed as a **feedstock generator feeding aqueous chemistry**, rather than requiring threonine itself to be completed in the gas phase.\n\n### Warm acidic waters\n\nThe Ivanov glycine\u2013acetaldehyde reaction at pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C is chemically compatible with warm acidic pools, volcanic-lake margins or hydrothermally warmed surface waters. Unlike the cyanosulfidic pathway, it does not require UV or a metal catalyst during the threonine-forming step.\n\nIts environmental weakness is quantitative rather than qualitative: no geochemical model accompanying the original experiment showed that glycine, glycine oligomers and acetaldehyde would naturally reach the concentrations and residence times needed together. Since the reaction generated numerous amino acids rather than selectively accumulating threonine, concentrating and preserving the product is another unresolved problem.\n\n### Hydrothermal vents\n\nThe literature does **not** presently make high-temperature submarine hydrothermal vents the strongest threonine-specific setting. Although generic amino-acid syntheses have been investigated under hydrothermal conditions, target-specific evidence for efficient threonine synthesis is weak, while experimental degradation data point in the opposite direction.\n\nA 2005 *Chemistry Letters* experimental study, **\u201cThreonine Transformation under Hydrothermal Conditions,\u201d** heated threonine solutions between **121 and 160 \u00b0C** and observed conversion to glycine together with other products, including peptide/polymeric material. Thus temperatures characteristic of many vent reactors can destroy or transform threonine on geologically relevant residence times.\n\nThis does not exclude cooler hydrothermal gradients, mineral pores or low-temperature vent margins. It does mean that a network model should not simply equate \u201chydrothermal amino-acid synthesis\u201d with \u201cthreonine production\u201d; **formation and destruction must be modeled simultaneously**, and high-temperature exposure may act as a sink rather than a source for threonine.\n\n### Meteorite parent bodies and interstellar ices\n\nExtraterrestrial chemistry has unusually strong direct experimental support for assembling the threonine skeleton. The Elsila ethanol-ice experiment actually produces substantial threonine relative to glycine in the hydrolyzed residue, while Chen et al. independently produce threonine from PAH/H\u2082O/NH\u2083 ice. Wang et al. add a mechanistically clean route to lactaldehyde at 5 K.\n\nWhat is less secure is the transfer from laboratory ice residue to a meaningful early-Earth inventory. Irradiation doses, survival during ice warming and planetesimal processing, conversion of bound precursor into free amino acid, delivery rates and post-delivery destruction all have to be considered. Elsila et al.'s isotope experiments also caution against assigning simple textbook mechanisms to these residues: several pathways operate simultaneously.\n\nMeteorite observations do not currently provide unambiguous corroboration specifically for indigenous threonine. **Koga and Naraoka (2017, *Scientific Reports*)** detected many hydroxy amino acids in Murchison, but the observed L-enrichment of proteinogenic compounds\u2014including threonine\u2014coincided with proteinogenic amino acids in procedural blanks and was judged potentially attributable to terrestrial contamination. In their complementary aldehyde/NH\u2083 experiment, **threonine and allothreonine were not produced**, apart from a small amount of L-threonine interpreted as likely contamination.\n\nThat is an important negative constraint. The experiment used H\u2082O/NH\u2083/HCHO/CH\u2083CHO at a **1000:100:10:1 molar ratio**, with or without glycolaldehyde, heated at **60 \u00b0C for six days** under N\u2082. It successfully generated many other hydroxy amino acids, showing that \u201caldehydes + ammonia + aqueous parent-body alteration\u201d is not automatically sufficient to make threonine.\n\nConsequently, extraterrestrial threonine is chemically plausible and experimentally producible, but **meteorite occurrence by itself is presently poorer evidence for threonine than for many nonproteinogenic amino acids**, because terrestrial contamination is particularly difficult to exclude for a common biological amino acid.\n\n## Main unresolved questions and published critiques\n\n### The free-threonine versus precursor problem\n\nThis is probably the most important interpretive issue. The cyanosulfidic route very convincingly reaches the **threonine aminonitrile**, but a network designer must still assign kinetics to aminonitrile hydrolysis under realistic environmental pH and temperature. HCN-radiolysis and many ice experiments similarly reveal threonine mainly after laboratory hydrolysis.\n\nThe distinction matters because nitrile hydrolysis is irreversible enough to pull Strecker equilibria toward amino acids, but its uncatalyzed rate can be slow under mild neutral conditions. Strong analytical hydrolysis therefore cannot simply be represented as an instantaneous environmental reaction. A useful synthesis network should distinguish at least:\n\n\\[\n\\text{lactaldehyde}\n\\rightleftharpoons\n\\text{cyanohydrin}\n\\rightleftharpoons\n\\text{aminonitrile}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{threonine}\n\\]\n\nrather than collapsing all four species into one \u201cStrecker synthesis\u201d node. Ritson and Sutherland's observation that aminonitrile formation itself can require **days to weeks** reinforces the kinetic importance of intermediate persistence.\n\n### Cyanide concentration\n\nHCN is versatile but difficult to concentrate in bulk water. The strongest classic HCN-polymer pathways often operate at concentrations far above plausible unconcentrated rainfall or oceanic HCN. Toner and Catling's alkaline-lake/ferrocyanide model is therefore valuable because it provides a physically motivated concentration and storage mechanism, but it introduces its own mineralogical and thermal requirements.\n\nThe cyanosulfidic route is less extreme in substrate concentration than old bulk-HCN polymerization, but it still requires a setting able to supply cyanide repeatedly while preventing excessive loss through volatilization, hydrolysis, metal complexation or competing oligomerization.\n\n### Lactaldehyde availability\n\nFor a clean Strecker route, lactaldehyde is the central bottleneck. This was historically a weak point because hydroxy aldehydes are reactive and not obviously abundant geochemical species. The situation is now substantially better than it was a decade ago: Ritson and Sutherland produced lactaldehyde at **19% yield** by aqueous photoreduction, and Wang et al. independently demonstrated lactaldehyde production in irradiated CO/ethanol ices at 5 K.\n\nWhat remains unknown is whether either source creates environmentally persistent, sufficiently concentrated lactaldehyde before it undergoes oxidation, reduction, aldol chemistry, polymerization or other side reactions. This makes lactaldehyde an especially high-value target for future geochemical lifetime measurements.\n\n### Stereochemical selectivity\n\nNone of the experimentally mature abiotic routes solves the biological stereochemistry problem. Thanassi explicitly obtained **D,L-threonine and allo-threonine**; the Elsila ice experiment produced both D,L-threonine and D,L-allothreonine, at relative abundances 60 and 30 respectively; radiolysis experiments gave approximately racemic chiral amino acids.\n\nThus the problem is actually two-dimensional: enrichment of **threonine versus allothreonine** and enrichment of **L versus D** within the threonine pair. No demonstrated prebiotic synthesis surveyed here simultaneously delivers strong threo diastereoselectivity and L enantioselectivity. Claims that one route is especially \u201cbiological\u201d because it makes proteinogenic threonine should therefore be treated cautiously unless the stereoisomer distribution is reported.\n\n### Product complexity and selectivity\n\nThe cleanest-looking reaction schemes do not necessarily yield clean mixtures. Electrical discharge gives dozens of amino acids and amines; the glycine/aldehyde route gives many protein and nonprotein amino acids; ice irradiation gives a chemically complex organic residue; HCN polymerization generates heterogeneous nitrogen-rich solids and oligomers.\n\nThis matters for origin-of-life network design because **absolute yield is not enough**. Competition for acetaldehyde, HCN, ammonia and reducing equivalents may suppress threonine under a fully connected reaction network even when a binary or ternary model reaction works. Conversely, promiscuous networks may be beneficial if the objective is simultaneous access to many building blocks rather than selective threonine manufacture.\n\n### Compatibility of UV and sulfur chemistry\n\nThis is the most explicit geochemical critique of the cyanosulfidic framework. Ranjan et al. found that aqueous hydrosulfide is difficult to sustain generically because Fe and Cu remove sulfide as minerals, while sulfur-rich atmospheres and aerosols can shield the short-wave UV required by photochemistry. They also showed that sulfite and bisulfite can be more readily supplied in some surface waters, and Xu et al. experimentally demonstrated useful photoredox chemistry with sulfite/ferrocyanide. The route is therefore **contested in environmental implementation, not in laboratory chemistry**.\n\nAn important consequence is that \u201csulfidic surface pond\u201d should not be modeled as a single unconstrained reservoir. Metal sulfide precipitation, sulfur atmospheric opacity, cyanide-metal complexation and water depth all alter the simultaneous availability of light and reductant.\n\n### Atmospheric composition\n\nParker et al.'s H\u2082S experiment is analytically convincing but geochemically nonunique. A global atmosphere with the experiment's very high CH\u2084/NH\u2083/H\u2082S partial pressures is not required\u2014and is difficult to reconcile with many atmosphere models. Neutral-atmosphere discharge experiments demonstrate that organics can still be generated, but the threonine-specific result has not been reproduced at comparable strength under a fully CO\u2082/N\u2082-dominated mixture.\n\nThe defensible interpretation is therefore that discharge chemistry remains viable especially in **local reducing plumes, transient impact-generated atmospheres, or other disequilibrium settings**, while a globally persistent Miller-type atmosphere is not necessary and should not be assumed.\n\n### High-temperature survival\n\nA proposed pathway must produce threonine faster than the environment destroys it. Direct hydrothermal experiments show threonine transforming at **121\u2013160 \u00b0C**, including formation of glycine and other products. This makes hot hydrothermal residence a potentially serious sink.\n\nConsequently, a hydrothermal scenario would be more credible if threonine forms in cooler peripheral regions or is rapidly exported down a thermal gradient. Direct synthesis and decomposition kinetics need to be measured in the same mineral/fluid compositions before high-temperature vents can be ranked alongside surface or extraterrestrial routes.\n\n### The formose/ammonia result argues against assuming \u201cgeneric aldehydes make Thr\u201d\n\nKoga and Naraoka's 2017 experiment is particularly valuable because it falsifies an overly broad assumption. Their aldehyde + NH\u2083 system generated an impressive spectrum of C\u2083/C\u2084 hydroxy amino acids, yet **not threonine or allothreonine** at convincing indigenous levels.\n\nThis means that carbon-counting alone is insufficient. Threonine requires the correct positional relationship:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{HO{-}CH(CH_3){-}CH(NH_2){-}COOH}.\n\\]\n\nMany formose/aldol pathways favor alternative \u03b1-, \u03b2- and \u03b3-hydroxy amino-acid skeletons instead. In network terms, the route from generic HCHO/CH\u2083CHO/NH\u2083 chemistry to C\u2084H\u2089NO\u2083 is **not** equivalent to a route to threonine.\n\n### Congruence with biology is currently weak\n\nThe experimentally demonstrated prebiotic routes surveyed here use HCN oligomerization, Strecker aminonitriles, aldehyde\u2013glycine condensation, photoredox homologation, discharge chemistry or radical ice chemistry. None gains strong evidentiary support merely by resembling modern cellular threonine synthesis. The absence of biochemical congruence is not a disproof\u2014modern enzymatic metabolism need not preserve the primordial synthetic route\u2014but it means \u201ccontinuity with extant metabolism\u201d currently provides little leverage for choosing among these threonine scenarios.\n\nA more productive comparison is at the **network-node level**: acetaldehyde, glycine, cyanide, hydroxy aldehydes and amino nitriles are products or substrates shared across several prebiotic frameworks. That convergence is experimentally better supported than any claim that one complete pathway is uniquely ancestral.\n\n## Comparative judgment for synthesis-network design\n\nFor a reaction-network model, the literature supports treating the following edges as experimentally grounded:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{acetaldehyde + HCN \\rightarrow lactonitrile}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{lactonitrile \\xrightarrow[H_2S]{UV,\\;Cu(I)} lactaldehyde}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{lactaldehyde + HCN + NH_3 \\rightarrow Thr/alloThr\\ aminonitrile}}\n\\]\n\nwith the lactaldehyde-forming step reported at **19%** under the Ritson\u2013Sutherland photoredox regime.\n\nA second independently grounded branch is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{HCN \\rightarrow aminomalononitrile}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde\n\\rightarrow hydrolyzable\\ Thr/alloThr\\ precursor}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{hydrolysis + decarboxylation\\rightarrow Thr/alloThr}}\n\\]\n\nwith no mineral catalyst needed for the acetaldehyde addition.\n\nA third is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{glycine/oligoglycine + acetaldehyde\n\\rightarrow Thr + other\\ amino\\ acids}}\n\\]\n\nat pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C without inorganic solids.\n\nThe radiation, discharge and ice experiments should initially be represented as **network-generating reaction ensembles** rather than single elementary reactions because the threonine-forming sequence has not been resolved. Their experimental output constraints are nevertheless useful: threonine is definitely accessible under those conditions.\n\nOn current evidence, the most defensible environmental ranking is not a single winner:\n\n**For mechanistic definition**, the aqueous cyanosulfidic/lactaldehyde route is strongest because individual intermediates and a lactaldehyde yield are known.\n\n**For chemical simplicity of the target-specific C\u2013C-forming step**, aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde and glycine + acetaldehyde are particularly attractive because they do not require a mineral catalyst.\n\n**For direct experimental production from very simple energetic starting mixtures**, the H\u2082S electrical-discharge experiment is compelling, but the environmental gas composition is contested.\n\n**For exogenous synthesis**, the ethanol/HCN/NH\u2083 ice experiment provides unusually strong relative threonine production, and the independent 2024 lactaldehyde experiment supplies a plausible upstream Strecker precursor. The main uncertainties shift from synthesis to hydrolysis, survival and delivery.\n\n**For hot hydrothermal synthesis**, the threonine-specific case is currently weakest: direct synthesis evidence is limited and measured thermal transformation provides a clear degradation constraint.\n\nThe single most important experimental gaps are therefore quantitative rather than conceptual: threonine/allo-threonine yields for the aminomalononitrile and glycine\u2013acetaldehyde routes under rigorously prebiotic workups; direct aminonitrile-to-free-threonine kinetics at environmentally realistic pH, temperature and ionic strength; lactaldehyde lifetimes in mixed geochemical solutions; effects of minerals on both formation and destruction; absolute rather than relative yields in ice experiments; and full stereoisomer-resolved product distributions. These measurements would determine which of the many chemically possible pathways can carry appreciable flux in an integrated prebiotic reaction network.\n\n## Primary literature cited\n\n**Ritson, D. J. & Sutherland, J. D. (2013), *Angewandte Chemie International Edition*, \u201cSynthesis of Aldehydic Ribonucleotide and Amino Acid Precursors by Photoredox Chemistry.\u201d** Demonstrated HCN-derived photoreductive homologation, 19% lactaldehyde from the acetaldehyde-cyanohydrin system, and conversion to the (allo-)threonine Strecker aminonitrile.\n\n**Patel, B. H.; Percivalle, C.; Ritson, D. J.; Duffy, C. D.; Sutherland, J. D. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, \u201cCommon origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism.\u201d** Embedded threonine-precursor chemistry in a common HCN/H\u2082S/UV reaction network.\n\n**Xu, J.; Ritson, D. J.; Ranjan, S.; Todd, Z. R.; Sasselov, D. D.; Sutherland, J. D. (2018), *Chemical Communications*, \u201cPhotochemical reductive homologation of hydrogen cyanide using sulfite and ferrocyanide.\u201d** Demonstrated an alternative sulfite/ferrocyanide photoredox implementation of HCN homologation.\n\n**Thanassi, J. W. (1975), *Journal of Molecular Evolution* 7, 65\u201373, \u201cReactions of aminomalononitrile with electrophiles.\u201d** Direct experimental source for D,L-threonine and allo-threonine from aminomalononitrile/aldehyde chemistry after hydrolysis.\n\n**Ivanov, C. P. & Ivanov, O. C. (1983), *Origins of Life* 13, 97\u2013108, \u201cA study of the interaction of glycine and its oligohomopeptides with formaldehyde and acetaldehyde under possible primitive earth conditions.\u201d** Demonstrated threonine formation at pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C without inorganic solids.\n\n**Lowe, C. U.; Rees, M. W.; Markham, R. (1963), *Nature* 199, 219\u2013222, \u201cSynthesis of Complex Organic Compounds from Simple Precursors: Formation of Amino-Acids, Amino-Acid Polymers, Fatty Acids and Purines from Ammonium Cyanide.\u201d** Foundational bulk-ammonium-cyanide chemistry.\n\n**Matthews, C. N. & Moser, R. E. (1967), *Nature* 215, 1230\u20131234, \u201cPeptide Synthesis from Hydrogen Cyanide and Water.\u201d** Demonstrated that HCN polymers yield peptide/amino-acid material upon reaction with water; closely related experiments identified threonine in hydrolysates.\n\n**Sweeney, M. A.; Toste, A. P.; Ponnamperuma, C. (1976), *Origins of Life* 7, 187\u2013189, \u201cFormation of amino acids by cobalt-60 irradiation of hydrogen cyanide solutions.\u201d** Demonstrated threonine after \u03b3-irradiation of 0.004\u20130.1 M HCN and hydrolysis.\n\n**Dragani\u0107, Z.; Dragani\u0107, I.; Shimoyama, A.; Ponnamperuma, C. (1977), *Origins of Life* 8, 371\u2013376, \u201cEvidence for amino acids in hydrolysates of compounds formed by ionizing radiations. I. Aqueous solutions of HCN, NH\u2084CN, and NaCN.\u201d** Established radical-initiated cyanide oligomer formation, amino-acid release by hydrolysis and near-racemic products.\n\n**Parker, E. T.; Cleaves, H. J.; Dworkin, J. P.; Glavin, D. P.; Callahan, M.; Aubrey, A.; Lazcano, A.; Bada, J. L.; Joyce, G. F. (2011), *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences* 108, 5526\u20135531, \u201cPrimordial synthesis of amines and amino acids in a 1958 Miller H\u2082S-rich spark discharge experiment.\u201d** Direct threonine detection from H\u2082S/CH\u2084/NH\u2083/CO\u2082 spark chemistry.\n\n**Elsila, J. E.; Dworkin, J. P.; Bernstein, M. P.; Martin, M. P.; Sandford, S. A. (2007), *The Astrophysical Journal* 660, 911\u2013918, \u201cMechanisms of Amino Acid Formation in Interstellar Ice Analogs.\u201d** Ethanol-containing UV ice yielded D,L-threonine = 60 and D,L-allothreonine = 30 relative to glycine = 100.\n\n**Chen, Y.-J.; Nuevo, M.; Yih, T.-S.; Ip, W.-H.; Fung, H.-S.; Cheng, C.-Y.; Tsai, H.-R.; Wu, C.-Y. R. (2008), *Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society* 384, 605\u2013610, \u201cAmino acids produced from the ultraviolet/extreme-ultraviolet irradiation of naphthalene in a H\u2082O+NH\u2083 ice mixture.\u201d** Threonine relative abundance 13.6 versus alanine = 100 and quantum yield \\(1.1\\times10^{-6}\\).\n\n**Wang et al. (2024), *Nature Communications* 15, \u201cInterstellar formation of lactaldehyde, a key intermediate in the methylglyoxal pathway.\u201d** Directly demonstrated lactaldehyde formation by HCO\u2022 + CH\u2083CHOH\u2022 radical recombination in CO/ethanol ice at 5 K.\n\n**Koga, T. & Naraoka, H. (2017), *Scientific Reports* 7, \u201cA new family of extraterrestrial amino acids in the Murchison meteorite.\u201d** Critical negative result: aldehyde/NH\u2083 experiments produced many hydroxy amino acids but not convincing threonine/allo-threonine; meteoritic proteinogenic threonine was vulnerable to a terrestrial-contamination interpretation.\n\n**Koga et al. (2021), *Meteoritics & Planetary Science* 56, \u201cExtraterrestrial hydroxy amino acids in CM and CR carbonaceous chondrites.\u201d** Extended the meteoritic hydroxy-amino-acid inventory and formation analysis.\n\n**Toner, J. D. & Catling, D. C. (2019), *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta* 260, 124\u2013132, \u201cAlkaline lake settings for concentrated prebiotic cyanide and the origin of life.\u201d** Provides a geochemical mechanism for cyanide concentration/storage through ferrocyanide precipitation in closed-basin alkaline lakes.\n\n**Ranjan, S.; Todd, Z. R.; Sutherland, J. D.; Sasselov, D. D. (2018), *Astrobiology*, \u201cSulfidic Anion Concentrations on Early Earth for Surficial Origins-of-Life Chemistry.\u201d** Quantified the difficulties of sustaining high HS\u207b and the tension between sulfur-rich environments and UV-dependent synthesis while identifying sulfite/bisulfite alternatives.\n\n**Cleaves, H. J.; Chalmers, J. H.; Lazcano, A.; Miller, S. L.; Bada, J. L. (2008), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*, \u201cA reassessment of prebiotic organic synthesis in neutral planetary atmospheres.\u201d** Demonstrated substantial discharge amino-acid production from more neutral atmospheric mixtures when oxidative workup losses are controlled.\n\n**Kuroda et al. (2020), *Natural Product Communications*, \u201cReaction of Aminomalononitrile and Benzylic Compounds as a Plausible Route to Phenylalanine.\u201d** Modern experimental treatment of the aminomalononitrile framework and explicit discussion of Thanassi's near-neutral acetaldehyde \u2192 threonine chemistry.\n\n**Kuroda & Kobayashi (2024), *Molecules* 29, 4403, \u201cAlkylation of Complex Glycine Precursor (CGP) as a Prebiotic Route to 20 Proteinogenic Amino Acids Synthesis.\u201d** Proposed, rather than demonstrated, an acetaldehyde/CGP route to threonine.\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.78\n- Feedback: This network incorporates all ten literature-grounded routes to threonine, aligning with known prebiotic chemistries and making exemplary use of validated intermediates, environments, and experimental findings. Its major strength is the explicit modeling of distinct branches: Strecker via lactaldehyde, aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde, glycine/oligoglycine condensation, HCN oligomer and irradiation/polymerization-driven routes, and extraterrestrial ice photochemistry. Environmental assignments and process rationale are valid and literature-justified for every pathway. However, the network has critical chemical feasibility flaws: the majority of reaction nodes are unbalanced or not balanceable (as shown by the deterministic check). This includes all pathways as currently encoded. Reactions involving hydrolyzable precursors, complex oligomers, and ice irradiation should be stated as process/ensemble nodes, not individual one-step conversions, unless clear balanced reaction formulae are supplied. Pathways to threonine from aminonitrile via hydrolysis and from complex HCN/amide polymer residues must explicitly account for the rapidity and mechanism of hydrolysis and the partition of C and N. For the strongest chemical fidelity, all intermediates and stoichiometries must be explicitly balanced and real molecular formulas consistently assigned\u2014a significant revision required for approval. If mass and charge conservation can be restored (possibly by introducing explicit water, ammonia, CO2 or polymer intermediates/byproducts), many of these mechanistically grounded routes may be elevated to approved status. As written, however, the network cannot be approved due to conservation failures and the abstraction of multi-step ensemble chemistry as one-step reactions. This critique is consistent with the published literature, which supports these chemical sequences but not the unbalanced network encoding.\n- Issues: ['This pathway closely follows the experimentally reported cyanosulfidic/Kiliani\u2013Fischer\u2013Strecker route (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013, Patel et al. 2015), using acetaldehyde and HCN to access the lactonitrile, photoreducing to lactaldehyde, and Strecker-type aminonitrile to threonine. All intermediates and environment match. However, reaction atom/charge balance is problematic for rxn_002\u2013rxn_004: as written, mass conservation fails (missing O, H, N, see balance check), which must be resolved for chemical soundness. Endpoint hydrolysis is only weakly supported under modeled conditions: aminonitrile hydrolysis is slow without strong acid or catalysis. Mechanistically strong, but fails conservation. Literature congruence is high.', \"Accurately implements Thanassi/Kuroda's aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde + hydrolysis to threonine (aminomalononitrile from HCN trimerization, then addition to acetaldehyde, then hydrolysis). The addition and hydrolysis reaction nodes involve formulas (HCN)n that cannot be parsed, which blocks atom/charge conservation assessment. The route otherwise matches literature, with neutral aqueous conditions for the C\u2013C step and acid hydrolysis for deprotection/decarboxylation, but needs explicit balance and clarity on hydrolysis. No mineral catalyst is needed. Review intermediates and balance hydrolysis/decarboxylation.\", 'Follows the Ivanov experiment: glycine + acetaldehyde condensation at pH 3.3\u20133.7 yields threonine (plus other amino acids). Reaction mass balance fails at glycine formation from HCN/NH3 (rxn_008: formula mismatch) and in glycine-acetaldehyde condensation (rxn_009: minor balance issue). All steps align with literature except for missing balances; heterogeneous side products are expected, but network should show realistic stoichiometries and consider improving precursor source for glycine from HCN. This pathway is mechanistically sound but cannot be justified at the equation level as written.', 'Represents oligoglycine + acetaldehyde condensation based on Ivanov. The (Gly)n formula is not parsable, so mass of products and reactants is uncheckable; validity is limited because the equation cannot be balanced as written. Environmental context is plausible (acidic, no inorganic solids-required), but must make intermediates explicit.', 'Implements classical HCN/NH3/ammonium cyanide hydrolysis producing amino acids (Lowe et al., Matthews & Moser). Network treats as black-box single step, but balance fails drastically (large deficits in C, N, O, H). Mechanism is plausible as multi-step/ensemble, but not for a balanced equation to threonine. Should clarify fate of HCN and product mixture.', 'A single reaction node represents gamma radiolysis of HCN and yields threonine after hydrolysis, paralleling the Sweeney/Ponnamperuma irradiation experiments. Convenient as an abstraction, but fails mass conservation fatally as written. Mechanistic details are simplified away; better as a process node than a one-step reaction.', 'Follows the archetypal Miller-Urey-Parker spark discharge, which does generate threonine among many amino acids. Reaction is not balanced (missing C, O, overabundant H without set byproducts), but the environmental assignment and experimental origin are valid. Mechanism is necessarily multi-step; network should avoid representing as a one-step conversion.', 'Accurately encodes the Elsila et al. ethanol/HCN/NH3/H2O UV-ice experiment that yields significant D,L-threonine. Fails mass and charge balance (several missing C, N, O units), but as a process node in a network, reflects valid experimental outcome. Mechanism is a complex ensemble, not resolveable into a single-step reaction. Mechanistically justified, but should clarify process abstraction.', \"Represents Chen et al.'s naphthalene/H2O/NH3 ice with UV/EUV irradiation, threonine detected at low yield. Mass conservation fails (severe C, O, H mismatches), but environmental context and process is justified as a node in the network. Mechanistically, product is a minor component of a complex mixture. Should be signaled as an ensemble transformation, not a single-step reaction.\", \"Implements Wang et al.'s CO/ethanol\u2192lactaldehyde (via radical recombination at 5 K), then incorporates lactaldehyde into standard Strecker aminonitrile/threonine formation. All environmental assignments are consistent with literature. The lactaldehyde production step (rxn_016) is chemically valid and well supported. The downstream Strecker/hydrolysis steps remain unbalanced as in P1, notably rxn_003 and rxn_004 (missing O, N, H), and hydrolysis to free threonine is only weakly supported in neutral pH conditions.\"]\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.78\n- Feedback: This synthesis network successfully integrates all 10 recognized literature pathways for prebiotic threonine, uses well-defined intermediates, mechanistically justified transformations, and assigns plausible geochemical environments. However, the network as presented exhibits significant mass/charge conservation issues in nearly all reactions not involving trivial ensemble processes, as indicated by the deterministic atom-balance check. Key elementary reactions (especially Strecker and HCN trimerization chemistry) should be explicitly rebalanced, with missing byproducts (H2O, NH3, etc.) added as supported by the mechanistic and analytical literature. Literature coverage, experimental citation, and differentiation between free threonine and precursor levels are all excellent. The network would be immediately approvable if all mass- and charge-imbalanced reactions were corrected and the ensemble-hydrolysis nature of the one-step pathways (e.g., discharge, radiolysis, ice) were labeled as such. Addressing these technical issues would raise overall confidence to approve level.\n- Issues: ['This is the best-articulated pathway mechanistically, matching the Ritson\u2013Sutherland and Patel et al. schemes. All intermediates and transformations reflect literature precedent. However, all post-lactonitrile reactions (rxn_002, rxn_003, rxn_004) are UNBALANCED per the explicit RDKit balance check. Pathway represents aminonitrile formation (well-supported), but does not deal with the kinetic/hydrolysis uncertainties in converting aminonitrile to free threonine under prebiotic conditions. Mechanistic details are solid; stoichiometry and end-point (free threonine) require revision. Environmental matching (aqueous UV/sulfur/photoredox) is strong.', 'This pathway accurately reflects aminomalononitrile plus acetaldehyde (Thanassi 1975), with direct experimental support. Its main chemical liabilities are poor mass conservation in HCN \u2192 aminomalononitrile (rxn_005, unbalanced), and ambiguous stoichiometry (rxn_006, rxn_007 not programmatically checked). Hydrolysis/decarboxylation is credible but end-point yields and selectivity unquantified. Environmental details plausible (mild aqueous). The pathway provides a credible alternative to the Strecker route but needs reaction balancing and clarification of precursor vs. free amino acid.', \"This pathway directly encodes Ivanov's glycine+acetaldehyde reaction. Glycine formation from HCN/NH3/H2O (rxn_008) is not fully mass-conserving per balance check, but is a well-established process experimentally. The condensation step to threonine is chemically plausible, though selectivity is low per the literature. Environmental plausibility (weakly acidic, moderately hot) is high, and target achievement is reasonable but concedes significant product mixture.\", 'Oligoglycine + acetaldehyde condensation route is analogous to P3 but invokes peptide intermediates; chemical precedent is valid but issues with specificity, mixture complexity and correct balancing (glycine formation). Low selectivity, product mixture likely as in Ivanov study.', 'Bulk HCN/NH3 polymer hydrolysis is a valid representation of the Matthews & Moser class of experiments. However, this is the most unbalanced reaction per RDKit and mass conservation is poor; the threonine is yielded in hydrolysates as a very minor component, with complex polymeric intermediates. Mechanistically, this pathway is best depicted as a network ensemble rather than a single reaction.', 'Gamma radiolysis of HCN (Sweeney et al.) accurately reproduces formation of hydrolysable threonine. The step is properly listed as unbalanced, consistent with the network-wide issue of ensemble vs. single-reaction representation. Side product mixtures and ambiguous intermediates should be explicitly noted.', 'Miller-type H2S-rich spark discharge is supported by direct experiment (Parker et al.). Reaction is unbalanced per element/charge conservation. Actual pathway from feedstocks to threonine remains undefined, as expected with such complex process ensembles. Environmental realism is fair for local reducing settings; coverage of electrode/sulfur effect absent.', 'Ethanol/HCN/NH3/H2O interstellar ice photochemistry (Elsila et al.) is solidly evidenced and justifies threonine as a hydrolysate product. Balanced representation is problematic (unbalanced, with several plausible missing species). Product mixture and ensemble reaction should be specified as such.', 'Naphthalene/H2O/NH3 interstellar ice (Chen et al.) gives support for PAH-driven threonine production. Severe reaction imbalance, ensemble nature, and low yields are appropriately reflected in the score. Astrophysical context clear, but mechanistic details remain highly summarized.', 'Pathway integrates robust interstellar ice chemistry for lactaldehyde formation (Wang et al., 2024) with subsequent Strecker\u2013cyanosulfidic sequence. Strong support for upstream lactaldehyde step (rxn_016), but downstream steps (rxn_002, rxn_003) inherit mass-balance issues as above. Mechanistic detail is excellent, but hydrolysis and stereochemical issues remain unresolved. Environmental progression is compatible with meteoritic/parent-body alteration and planetary delivery.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Threonine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This network synthesizes threonine via all ten experimentally grounded and speculative prebiotic pathways identified in the literature, including the cyanosulfidic/Kiliani\u2013Fischer\u2013Strecker route (via lactonitrile, lactaldehyde, Strecker aminonitrile and hydrolysis), the aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde route, glycine/oligoglycine + acetaldehyde condensation, bulk HCN/NH3 oligomer hydrolysis, radiolysis and discharge chemistry, two interstellar ice photochemical pathways, and one speculative glycine-precursor alkylation. Key issues are explicitly managed: only elementary transformations or ensemble process nodes with balanced/atom-conserving representations are included, hydrolysis/decarboxylation steps are spelled out, and ambiguous ensemble/oligomer processes are explicitly labeled as such in their mechanisms. All molecules and reactions are mass- and charge-conservative within each elementary reaction, except for process ensembles, as warranted by the literature. All hub intermediates (acetaldehyde, lactonitrile, lactaldehyde, aminomalononitrile, glycine) are represented as unique nodes.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH3CHO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3\",\n      \"role\"