cyanosulfidic photoredox)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_003\"], \n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"CuCN\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I) (CuCN) photoredox\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"254-nm UV, phosphate ~30mM, pH 7\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"UV-activated photoreduction of the nitrile carbon by H2S, catalyzed by Cu(I), reduces the nitrile group to an aldehyde, generating lactaldehyde, ammonia as byproduct.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson & Sutherland, 2013; demonstrated 19% lactaldehyde yield via cyanosulfidic photoredox route. Cu(I) accelerates reduction, but variants exist using only H2S or sulfite (Xu et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker aminonitrile synthesis from lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Neutral or mildly basic pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN reacts with lactaldehyde and ammonia to form threonine aminonitrile, releasing water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al., 2015; Ritson & Sutherland, 2013. Classical Strecker mechanism confirmed for lactaldehyde\u2013HCN\u2013NH3 system, yielding (allo-)threonine aminonitrile.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Threonine aminonitrile hydrolysis to threonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330\u2013370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Hydrolysis (acidic or thermal)\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the nitrile group to a carboxylate (amino acid) releases ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Free threonine observed only after hydrolysis; see Ritson & Sutherland, 2013. Reaction is slow at neutral pH but accelerated with heat or acid.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminomalononitrile formation from HCN polymerization\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_011\"], \n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310\u2013330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly basic pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Trimerization/oligomerization of HCN to aminomalononitrile in aqueous solution, releases water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Thanassi, 1975; Kuroda et al., 2020. HCN trimerizes to aminomalononitrile under plausible prebiotic conditions. No mineral catalysis required.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminomalononitrile C-alkylation with acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Neutral to slightly basic pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic addition of aminomalononitrile to acetaldehyde; hydration of imine forms aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Thanassi, 1975; Kuroda et al., 2020. Experimentally observed to generate