m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Threonine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: This network synthesizes threonine via all ten experimentally grounded and speculative prebiotic pathways identified in the literature, including the cyanosulfidic/Kiliani\u2013Fischer\u2013Strecker route (via lactonitrile, lactaldehyde, Strecker aminonitrile and hydrolysis), the aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde route, glycine/oligoglycine + acetaldehyde condensation, bulk HCN/NH3 oligomer hydrolysis, radiolysis and discharge chemistry, two interstellar ice photochemical pathways, and one speculative glycine-precursor alkylation. Key issues are explicitly managed: only elementary transformations or ensemble process nodes with balanced/atom-conserving representations are included, hydrolysis/decarboxylation steps are spelled out, and ambiguous ensemble/oligomer processes are explicitly labeled as such in their mechanisms. All molecules and reactions are mass- and charge-conservative within each elementary reaction, except for process ensembles, as warranted by the literature. All hub intermediates (acetaldehyde, lactonitrile, lactaldehyde, aminomalononitrile, glycine) are represented as unique nodes.\n\nMOLECULES (18 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CH3CHO (acetaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_002: HCN (hydrogen cyanide)